  "notes": {
    "part:Q1": "Q1 is biased by its own leakage (no resistor from its base to the supply): a leaky part is needed. Try 50 µA and 300 µA measured.",
    "part:TR1": "Q2 bias: set first; on the pedal, Aion sets it until Q3's collector reads -8 to -8.5 V. It moves Q2 and Q3 together. Here it is solved for Q2's collector at -0.16 V, inside Aion's readings: with the OC75 card, aiming at Q3's collector leaves Q2 outside them (D-184). Aion's addition, one of this version's differences from the original: Fuzz Central's trace of an original has a fixed 100K on Q2's collector and 8K2 alone on Q3's (the starter Tone Bender MkII).",
    "part:TR2": "Q3 bias: fine-tunes Q3's collector after TR1. Aion's addition: the original has 8K2 alone here.",
    "part:R6": "Feedback from Q3's emitter to Q2's base, as in a Fuzz Face.",
    "part:VR2": "With C5, bypasses Q3's emitter: more Fuzz, more gain.",
    "part:SW1A": "Mode: MkII (Q1's boost stage in front) or 1.5 (the guitar straight into Q2 through C4, Q1 left out). Only capacitors switch, so the bias is the same in both.",
    "part:C4": "In MkII mode C4 floats, and in 1.5 mode C3 and C2 do: switching modes can pop (the Missing pull-down warnings). Aion's board has no pull-downs here; 1M to ground on each would stop it, but shifts Q2's bias a little (D-135)."
  },
